---
import Project from "@layouts/Project.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    title: "Go Quest",
    coverImage: "/deathandrevival/Landscape 2.jpg",
    coverImageAlt: "Go Quest",
};
---

<Project
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Playable Immersive Environment Exploring Spatial Logic and Sensory Interaction"
    description=""
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio VII"
    courseDescription="Inception"
    instructor="Carla Leitao"
    collaborators="Odette Gonzalez & Sarah Lowrey"
    date="Fall 2023"
    location="Troy, New York"
>
    <section class="main">
        <p>
            Go Quest originated as an exploration of immersive space—how
            environments can shape perception, emotion, and behavior. Early
            studies examined dreams, sensory distortion, and the psychology of
            fear, using AI-generated imagery to visualize dream fragments and
            test how abstraction, atmosphere, and ambiguity influence emotional
            response. These experiments led to investigations into discomfort
            and restlessness, drawing inspiration from unsettling media such as
            the “I'm Poppy” video, whose repetitive calmness and uncanny tone
            produced visible unease among viewers. Additional research examined
            immersive simulations of mental disorders, including games designed
            to approximate the perceptual distortions experienced by individuals
            with schizophrenia. The team also studied the anechoic “room of
            silence,” a space so acoustically absorbent that the absence of
            stimuli causes the brain to generate its own—an effect that induces
            anxiety in some and calm in others.
        </p>
        <p>
            These independent explorations formed the conceptual foundation for
            a project centered on immersion as a spatial condition, not merely a
            visual effect. When the team transitioned into collaborative
            development, the question became: How can immersion be constructed,
            controlled, and manipulated through interaction? With access to the
            Craive Lab—a research facility equipped with high-end 360°
            projection technology—the project naturally shifted toward digital
            methods. Unity became the medium through which spatial ideas could
            be tested, refined, and ultimately experienced as a fully
            interactive environment.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Precedents and Influences</h3>
        <p>
            Three key references shaped the conceptual and experiential
            framework of Go Quest:
        </p>

        <div class="gallery">
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Cinque Terre"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Borderless — Inviting Chaos</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    TeamLab's Borderless introduced the idea of environments
                    that behave independently of the user. Through projections,
                    mirrors, and LED-driven infinity effects, the installation
                    creates a world where elements move, react, and evolve
                    without direct player control. This sense of boundlessness
                    and unpredictability informed Go Quest's approach to
                    environmental autonomy.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Forest"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Dark Echo — Trial and Error</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Dark Echo inspired the use of darkness, sound, and discovery
                    through action. In a world without visual cues, players
                    navigate by producing sound that reveals spatial boundaries.
                    This mechanic—akin to echolocation—reinforced the idea that
                    exploration should be learned, not instructed. Go Quest
                    adopts this philosophy by allowing players to uncover the
                    world piece by piece.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Northern Lights"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Monument Valley 2 — Contained Tools</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Monument Valley demonstrated the power of compact, legible
                    stages where players experiment with tools to progress. Its
                    clarity and controlled puzzle logic offered a counterpoint
                    to Borderless's chaos. Go Quest positions itself between
                    these extremes, blending unpredictability with moments of
                    agency.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
    <br />

    <section>
        <h3>From Research to Mechanics</h3>
        <p>
            The final game world is built from a field of modular box tiles that
            function as both spatial units and event triggers. Each tile type
            embodies a specific behavior, creating a system where interaction
            generates complexity:
        </p>
        <div class="gallery">
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Cinque Terre"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Basic Tile</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    The core spatial unit; its glowing edges orient the player
                    in darkness.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Forest"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Light Tile</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Illuminates on contact, mapping the player's path and
                    forming a spatial memory.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Northern Lights"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Trap Tile</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Vanishes when stepped on, opening a void that may cause the
                    player to fall and respawn.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Northern Lights"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Colored Tiles</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Encode progress through shifting hues, creating a visual
                    hierarchy tied to movement and time.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <img
                    src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                    alt="Northern Lights"
                    width="600"
                    height="400"
                />
                <h4 class="title">Reactive Tiles</h4>
                <p class="desc">
                    Activate, resize, or extrude other tiles, revealing new
                    platforms and expanding the navigable world.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p>
            This taxonomy functions like an architectural material palette—each
            element has a behavior, a purpose, and a role in shaping the
            environment.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Immersion and Sensory Design</h3>
        <div>
            <p style="display: block; width: 70%; padding-right:15px">
                Sound plays a central role in constructing immersion.
                Atmospheric audio establishes tone, while reactive sound effects
                respond to player movement, reinforcing the connection between
                user and environment. Pulsating rhythms mark the passage of
                time, and subtle hums from glowing tiles create a layered
                soundscape that deepens presence. The interplay of darkness,
                illumination, and sound transforms navigation into a sensory
                experience rather than a purely visual one.
            </p>
            <img
                src={frontmatter.coverImage}
                alt="Northern Lights"
                style="display: block; width: 30%; padding-left:15px"
                width="600"
                height="400"
            />
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Gameplay and Spatial Evolution</h3>
        <div>
            <p>
                Players begin in darkness, illuminated only by a grid of tiles.
                Each step lights the tile beneath them, creating a glowing trace
                that records their movement. Tile functions activate on contact,
                layering interaction over exploration. Reaching the periphery
                triggers the first major event: the revelation of spatial bounds
                beyond the ground plane.
            </p>
            <p>
                Players begin in darkness, illuminated only by a grid of tiles.
                Each step lights the tile beneath them, creating a glowing trace
                that records their movement. Tile functions activate on contact,
                layering interaction over exploration. Reaching the periphery
                triggers the first major event: the revelation of spatial bounds
                beyond the ground plane.
            </p>
            <p>
                By the time all triggers activate, the relationship between
                player and world has fundamentally changed. What began as a
                predictable environment becomes a complex, shifting system that
                challenges perception, memory, and control.
            </p>
        </div>

        <section>
            <h3>Immersive Presentation</h3>
            <div>
                <p>
                    The final version of Go Quest was presented in the Craive
                    Lab's 360° projection environment, allowing the game to be
                    experienced as a spatial installation. The surrounding
                    projections amplified the game's atmospheric qualities,
                    immersing players in a world where sound, light, and
                    movement enveloped them from all sides. This setting
                    reinforced the project's origins in immersive research and
                    demonstrated how digital environments can operate as
                    architectural experiences.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Conclusion</h3>
            <div>
                <p>
                    What began as an abstract study of dreams, emotions, and
                    sensory immersion evolved into a fully interactive
                    Unity-built world. Go Quest merges architectural thinking
                    with game design, using spatial logic, behavioral systems,
                    and sensory cues to construct an environment that unfolds
                    through exploration. It stands as both a game and a spatial
                    experiment—an investigation into how immersion can be
                    designed, manipulated, and experienced.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </section>
</Project>
